# The screen only lights up for a real question

## Story

As a sales rep on a live call,
I want the second screen to light up only for a genuine technical question from the prospect and stay dark for everything else,
so that a quick glance is always worth taking.

## What this is

This is the gatekeeper for the screen. Most of what's said on a call should
never trigger anything — greetings, chit-chat about the weekend, the rep's own
talking, plain statements, scheduling, and anything about pricing. The screen
should only wake up when the prospect genuinely asks a technical question we
could actually answer from our product docs.

It leans deliberately toward staying quiet. When it isn't sure, it doesn't
fire. A wrong card flashing up during small talk erodes the rep's trust in the
screen far more than quietly missing the occasional question ever would. So
whenever it's a close call, the answer is "stay dark."

## What "done" looks like

- It fires only for a real technical question coming from the prospect — even
  if the prospect happens to be directing it at a colleague rather than the
  rep. It never fires on the rep's own words.
- Greetings, small talk, plain statements that aren't questions, and
  back-channel noise ("mm-hmm," "right") never fire.
- Pricing and scheduling never fire — those conversations belong to the rep,
  not the screen.
- Each question is answered only once per call. If essentially the same
  question comes up again later in the same call, the screen stays dark the
  second time. There's a clean way to start fresh for a new call, so the
  "already asked" memory never carries over between calls.
- The obvious cases — clearly not a question, clearly the rep talking, clearly
  a repeat of something already asked — are dismissed instantly, without
  spending any real effort thinking about them. Only the genuinely ambiguous
  lines get the slower, more careful look.
- It never crashes. If anything at all goes wrong while it's deciding, it
  simply treats that as "stay dark."
- Every decision leaves one short, quiet note in the running log — what was
  said (shown safely, so nothing in the spoken words can garble or take over
  the log) and whether it fired and why.
- For rehearsal and testing, there's a practice mode with fixed, predictable
  decisions that needs no outside help and no network.
- Because spotting a repeat matters so much, there's an option to lean on a
  shared memory to catch questions asked in slightly different words — but it
  always keeps working, falling back to its own judgment, even when that shared
  memory isn't available.

There's a fixed set of example lines, each labelled with what should happen,
kept as the accuracy yardstick. Running straight through them, it should get
nearly all of them right — at least eighteen out of every twenty — and it must
get every single small-talk, pricing, and scheduling line right by staying
dark. Those are the ones that must never, ever fire.
